### Summary

Recent-history reconciliation reads only twice the number of platform candidates in canonical user/assistant text rows, so an intervening internal runtime input/NO_REPLY pair can push an already-answered candidate out of the lookup and cause it to reappear in later Discord/Codex context.

### Steps to reproduce

The following executed fixture isolates the failure with no steering or consecutive-user ambiguity:

1. Provide six unchanged, ID-bearing recent-channel candidates: `target`, then `newer-1` through `newer-5`.
2. Give each candidate its own directly following completed assistant answer. This produces 12 chronological canonical text rows. Reconcile: all six candidates are omitted.
3. Insert one internal user-role runtime input and its assistant `NO_REPLY` between the target pair and the five newer pairs. Keep the same six platform candidates and all six completed external answers. There are now 14 canonical text rows.
4. Run the production `mergeSessionTranscriptContext` recent-history path with `historyLimit:20`.
5. It still asks for only 12 rows and retains `target` in `InboundHistory`.
6. As a coverage-only control, return 14 rows to the unchanged algorithm: all six candidates are omitted again.

The script below executes these cases against the production owner module, replacing only transcript I/O and the unrelated command classifier. It does not rewrite the reconciliation algorithm.

### Expected behavior

Unrelated internal runtime activity should not make a still-selected, unchanged, answered platform message reappear merely by consuming a candidate-count-based evidence budget.

The six-pair control demonstrates the intended omission. The documentation at [docs/channels/discord/threads-and-sessions.md:63-65](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/489774034984cd3ef78706125f20f4b25723288b/docs/channels/discord/threads-and-sessions.md#L63-L65) explicitly qualifies suppression with bounded-transcript confirmation. This report identifies a concrete coverage weakness of that bound; it does not claim a guarantee of unlimited-history deduplication or request unbounded scans.

### Actual behavior

Executed results:

```text
Fixture                              candidates  canonical rows  requested limit  returned coverage  retained
Six ordinary answered pairs          6           12              12               12 rows            []
Same pairs + internal U/NO_REPLY      6           14              12               12 rows            [target]
Same 14 rows, coverage-only control   6           14              12               14 rows            []
```

In the historical November 11 incident, the preceding 20 physical Discord messages yielded six human candidates. The corresponding canonical tail included five newer user/final pairs plus an internal runtime input and `NO_REPLY`. The older candidate and its completed answer were respectively 14th and 13th from the tail, outside the 12-row read. That answered message then appeared in the native `Recent chat history` block. A read-only reconstruction with 14 rows recognized it.

The synthetic reproduction needs no steering: each external input has an adjacent completed answer. Only evidence coverage changes between the failing case and its control.

