- Severity: context-quality/efficiency defect, not a crash or lost delivered reply.
- Frequency: one historical multi-input incident investigated; the fixed synthetic fixture reproduces deterministically. No population-wide rate measured.
- Consequence: an already-answered request is appended again inside a later model input, alongside conversation history that already contains it. Token cost and subsequent answer-quality effects were not measured.

### Additional information

#### Source explanation

[src/channels/inbound-event/session-transcript-context.runtime.ts:55-108](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/489774034984cd3ef78706125f20f4b25723288b/src/channels/inbound-event/session-transcript-context.runtime.ts#L55-L108) scans projected rows backward. A completed assistant sets `replied=true`; the next preceding user gets entered in `answered`; every user resets `replied=false`. In `original -> steering -> final`, only steering gets completion credit.

This is deliberately conservative. The source explains:

> A reply proves only the user row right before it; earlier rows in the same gap (a failed turn, or collected extras) stay, since a duplicate beats lost context.

That protects genuinely unanswered earlier inputs, but the current projection cannot distinguish those from the original input of the same completed steered run. [src/config/sessions/transcript-recent-text.ts:114-133](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/489774034984cd3ef78706125f20f4b25723288b/src/config/sessions/transcript-recent-text.ts#L114-L133) supplies transport identity and completion classification, not accepted-steering/run membership. The existing `steerTargetRunId` metadata owner is [src/sessions/user-turn-transcript.metadata.ts:120-135](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/489774034984cd3ef78706125f20f4b25723288b/src/sessions/user-turn-transcript.metadata.ts#L120-L135); using it is a possible implementation direction, not a tested solution or proof that every accepted input was semantically answered.

#### Repair acceptance criteria

- Cover the original input plus accepted steering when completion evidence establishes the same logical run covered them.
- Do not simply remove the reset and classify every earlier user as answered. Preserve failed/no-output earlier runs and unrelated unanswered discussion.
- Preserve exact message-ID/body comparison, edits, attachment caution, session/agent scope, and incomplete-output retention.
- Add a regression where all projected evidence fits and a single-input control, plus the existing earlier-failed-input guard at [src/channels/inbound-event/session-transcript-context.sqlite.test.ts:151-182](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/489774034984cd3ef78706125f20f4b25723288b/src/channels/inbound-event/session-transcript-context.sqlite.test.ts#L151-L182).
- Verify the resulting next native input through a composed channel/Gateway test when implementing a repair.

#### Status and evidence limits

The omission owner and private transcript projector were also read from upstream `main` at `6c32a15729f269d6ed44b89713eb6ae004f15ab1`; both match the tested files. This is source compatibility, not a deployed-main live test.

The executable fixture runs the exact production omission function with a controlled reader. It does not exercise steering admission, persistence, or a real model; those aspects come from the historical incident. No known-good multi-input release was established, so this is not labeled a regression. No code/configuration changes or new live test messages were made.

Duplicate triage distinguished reports about **unanswered** steering inputs or skipped message-tool deliveries: here the combined answer was delivered and persisted, and the defect is later history attribution.

