Closes #198021

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: ElevenLabs Scribe transcripts routed through OpenRouter keep audio-event tags such as `(jingle)` or `(laughter)` even when `tag_audio_events: false` is configured, because the OpenRouter speech-to-text adapter only sends `temperature` from `providerOptions` and drops every other key.

## User Impact

User impact: audio-event tags can be turned off for OpenRouter Scribe transcription, including Talk, with this config:

```json5
{
  tools: {
    media: {
      models: [
        { provider: "openrouter", model: "elevenlabs/scribe-v2", capabilities: ["audio"] },
      ],
      audio: {
        enabled: true,
        providerOptions: {
          openrouter: { tag_audio_events: false },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

The same key also works on a single audio entry in `tools.media.models[]`. `providerOptions.elevenlabs` keeps applying only to the direct ElevenLabs provider.

**Limit:** the upstream provider's slug comes from the model prefix (`elevenlabs/scribe-v2` → `elevenlabs`). OpenRouter forwards `provider.options.<slug>` only to the provider that serves the request, so the options are dropped when a provider with a different slug serves the model (for example an `openai/whisper-*` model served by another host). Nested `providerOptions` values, which would let users write OpenRouter's `provider.options.<slug>` object directly, would be the full fix. That needs a config-schema and plugin SDK change and is left out of this PR.

## Why This Change Was Made

OpenRouter's `/audio/transcriptions` request takes provider-specific passthrough options under `provider.options.<provider-slug>`. OpenClaw's `providerOptions` values are flat (string, number, boolean or string array), so the adapter keeps `temperature` as a top-level field and sends every other `providerOptions.openrouter` key under `provider.options.<model prefix>`. Requests without extra options are unchanged. The OpenRouter provider docs describe the setting and the limit.

## Evidence

- New test in `extensions/openrouter/media-understanding-provider.test.ts`: with `elevenlabs/scribe-v2` and `{ tag_audio_events: false, temperature: 0 }`, the request body carries `temperature: 0` and `provider: { options: { elevenlabs: { tag_audio_events: false } } }`. It fails without the adapter change (body has no `provider` field) and passes with it.
- `extensions/openrouter/media-understanding-provider.test.ts`: 16 passed.
- Extensions typecheck (`tsconfig.extensions.json`): no errors. `oxlint` and `oxfmt --check` clean on the changed files.
- Talk's file transcription goes through the same media-understanding runner, so it gets the same request.
- Not run live: no request was sent to OpenRouter, so Scribe dropping the tags end to end has not been observed.

Fixes #176028

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: on turns whose visible reply must go through the `message` tool (`message_tool_only`), a message that arrives while the agent is working can make steering skip the `message(action=send)` that carries the finished answer, so the earlier message never gets its reply.

## User Impact

User impact: a reply the agent already wrote is delivered even when the user sends another message mid-turn. The newer message is still handled right after, in order, as the steering docs promise. Users who type follow-ups while the agent works no longer see answers silently disappear.

## Why This Change Was Made

Since #163852, steering skips the unstarted tail of a sequential tool batch once one call has started. That is the right call for work the model may want to replan, but on `message_tool_only` turns the final text is private and the answer is itself a tool call. Any sequential sibling (bundled MCP tools are sequential by default) made the answer skippable.

The steering checkpoint in agent-core now asks an internal, OpenClaw-owned classifier whether the call about to launch delivers the turn's visible reply, and does not skip it when it does. The steer stays queued and is applied after that call's result. The classification lives with the existing message-tool terminal hook, which already owns "a current-source send is this turn's reply" after delivery; it now answers the same question before launch, reusing the same route helpers:

- only `message` send actions, only on `message_tool_only` turns;
- sends with no explicit route, or whose explicit route resolves to the current conversation;
- sends to other conversations, non-send actions, other tools, and automatically delivered turns are unchanged.

Running tools, parallel batches, and later calls in the batch keep their current behavior. If steering already skipped an earlier call in the same batch, the send is skipped with the rest of the batch: that answer was composed assuming the skipped call would run, so the model replans with both skips visible. Docs updated in `concepts/queue-steering`, `concepts/queue`, and `concepts/agent`.

Accepted edge case: classification reads the call's arguments as the model sent them, before `before_tool_call` hooks or preparers can rewrite them, and a `dryRun` or `final: false` progress send to the current conversation also counts. In either case the only effect is that the call launches one step before the steer applies.

## Evidence

Gateway proof (real Gateway process from the built runtime, real plugin loading, `chat.send` over the Gateway protocol, session transcript read back with `chat.history`; only the model is scripted). New case in `test/gateway-steer-fifo.e2e.test.ts`: a `message_tool_only` webchat turn answers input A with `[steering_gate (sequential, held), message(send A), steering_tail]`; input B steers in while the gate is held; the model then answers B with `message(send B)`. The case asserts:

- the first request's system prompt is the message-tool-only one (final text private, so the send is the only visible answer);
- the send's tool result reaching the model is "Sent visible reply to the current source conversation", not the steering skip;
- the ordinary `steering_tail` call is still skipped, the steer follows the tool results, and the tail tool never executes;
- `chat.history` shows answer A, then answer B.

Captured from one run of that case with this change: the scripted provider's second request (what the Gateway sent back after the first batch), the fixture plugin's execution trace, and `chat.history` for the session as the WebChat user sees it. The capture was a temporary local dump in the test, not committed. Redaction: user entries are trimmed to the message text (sender metadata and timestamps removed); nothing else was edited.

