## What Problem This Solves

Fixes “No answer was returned” appearing after a progress message has already delivered the text that the model subsequently repeats as its final answer.

The trigger is `message(action: "send", final: false)`, followed by a successful, nonempty automatic final with the same text and destination. `final: false` matters: a completed source send (`final: true` or omitted) can end the turn before this path is reached.

## User Impact

The delivered answer remains visible once, and the turn completes without a false missing-reply notice. Sending to another destination still preserves the automatic answer in the originating chat. Pending delivery, observer failures, blocked or unfinished turns, deliberate silence, and message-tool-only completion retain their existing authority.

## Why This Change Was Made

The runtime can report `sourceReplyDeliveryState: "missing"` before the automatic final is processed. Route-and-content deduplication subsequently confirms that this final was already delivered, but the old completion guard ignored that evidence whenever the runtime supplied any delivery state.

The existing deduplication callback now reconciles confirmed delivery in both immediate and queued replies. A shared guard accepts `missing` only for successful, noncontinuing automatic turns and preserves pending delivery custody. No SDK, configuration, or migration changes are required.

This is independent of #195842: the reproduction below uses ordinary chats without topic IDs, and this branch contains only the completion-accounting fix.

## Evidence

- Four regression assertions fail before the fix; 111 targeted tests pass afterward across immediate and queued replies, including the negative completion-custody cases.
- Full package build, core type checking, targeted lint/format, test-line budget, and commit hooks passed for `5e182fca5c96924fd185a732d809ae440f3fda1b`.
- [CI, dependency review, and security-sensitive review passed](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/42605677128) for this head.
- The built-Gateway traces below capture the actual message-tool arguments, Bot API requests and successful responses, model final output, and the nonempty final read back through `gateway call chat.history`.

### Gateway reproduction

Executed November 9, 2026. Each case starts an isolated built Gateway with fresh state, Telegram polling, automatic replies, streaming disabled, and only the message tool enabled. An inbound `getUpdates` message enters the real Telegram plugin. The deterministic model fixture first emits a `message` call with `final: false`, then an identical nonempty final. The Gateway performs tool execution, delivery recording, final processing, deduplication, and completion accounting.

The Telegram Bot API and model endpoints are local fixtures; these traces are not live Telegram or live-model evidence. Chat IDs and text are synthetic. No delivery or completion result is injected into the Gateway. Network observations come from the fixture endpoints; `gateway_persisted_final` is read back from Gateway history. Its projection retains role, text, provider, API, stop reason, and timestamp and omits incidental usage/identity metadata.

The before build is `c99a0840418571a09d1a1347d8005ce207cd271f`, which contains the separate topic-matching patch but no completion fix. Its three completion-owner source files are byte-identical to the merge base `9a26486062c8`; this reproduction supplies no topic IDs. Both after cases use exactly this PR head, `5e182fca5c96924fd185a732d809ae440f3fda1b`, without #195842.

| Case | Tool destination | Nonempty final persisted | Answer deliveries | Missing-reply notice |
