Closes #196788

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: on a Gateway with two or more chat channels (for example Telegram and Discord), adding a Template whose automation posts its result to "the last channel" creates the agent but not the automation.

## User Impact

User impact: Add now creates every Template automation in the new agent's main session, with no channel delivery, so it works the same on every Gateway and the automation's results show up in the agent's own chat. To post results to a channel or run an automation in an isolated session, change it in the automation's settings after Add.

This is a deliberate behavior change for all Gateways, not only multi-channel ones: a Template automation that used to post to the last channel on a single-channel Gateway now stays in the agent's chat. Template authors don't need to change anything: `CLAW.md` still requires `session` and accepts `delivery`, Add just doesn't apply them.

## Why This Change Was Made

A Template automation can only send its result to "the last channel" (the chat the agent was last used in). When several channels are configured, the scheduler refuses that for an automation that runs in its own isolated session, because it can't tell which channel is meant. On main, Add then creates the agent and shows "Automation setup failed".

An earlier revision of this PR asked the person adding the Template to pick a channel. Following the Template owner's direction in the Agent Templates Focus Week thread (Patrick Erichsen), this version is simpler: Add ignores the declared `session` and `delivery` and schedules each declared automation in the agent's main session with no delivery. That keeps a Template agent to one conversation, which is where the Templates UX is heading, and the automation never reaches the scheduler's multi-channel rule. The channel picker, the `automationChannel` request field and the scheduler refactor from the earlier revision are gone; the change is now the Add planner, one new test, one updated test expectation and the docs.

Left as is:

- Automations converted from a Template's legacy heartbeat input (`agent.heartbeat` / `HEARTBEAT.md`) keep their current behavior (owner delivery, and `isolatedSession` still honored). They never used last-channel delivery.
- The Gateway review response schema still lists `last-channel` as a delivery value; Add no longer produces it. Removing it would be a protocol change, so I left it for a follow-up if wanted.

## Evidence

**Regression test with the real scheduler.** `src/templates/add-stateless.automations.test.ts` adds a Template with an isolated, last-channel automation through the real Add installer and `CronService`, with the scheduler reporting two configured channels (Discord and Telegram).

- On main, Add ends `partial` with the scheduler's error: "cron announce delivery requires an explicit channel when multiple channels are configured (discord, telegram)".
- On this branch, Add is `complete`, and the job is created in `session:agent:watcher:main` with `delivery: { mode: "none" }`.
- Undoing only the session override makes the test fail again.

**Real Gateway with Telegram and Discord configured**, Add through `templates.add.plan` and `templates.add.apply`. Built from this branch (`58c7ccfaff6`; the later commit only changes docs and a code comment) and from main (`c87a2ddd751`), with local stand-ins for ClawHub and the model, so nothing leaves the machine:

| | main | this branch |
|---|---|---|
| Add result | `partial` / `add_failed` with the scheduler error above; the agent is kept | `complete` |
| Automation in `cron.list` | none | `session:agent:incident-lead:main`, `delivery: none` |

Also on this branch:

- A forced run of the automation finishes `ok` with `deliveryStatus: not-requested`, and its result shows up in the agent's main chat (`chat.history`).
- With one channel configured, Add is also `complete` and creates the same main-session automation.
- Control: a direct `cron.add` of an isolated last-channel job is still rejected on the two-channel Gateway, so the scheduler rule itself is unchanged.

The stand-in model answers every prompt with the same sentence, which is why the automation's result reads like the agent's greeting.

**Control UI**, same two-channel setup.

Before (main `0f0c5f0f6e5` plus the unrelated #196714 change, captured for the earlier revision): the agent is created and the chat shows "Automation setup failed: heartbeat-summary".

![Before: main creates the agent without its automation](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8a0e022e-abc3-4759-a6fd-9d902d7ce939)

After (this branch): Add goes straight to the new agent's chat, with no extra question, and shows "Automation created: Incident heartbeat summary."

![After: the automation is created in the agent's main session](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f0fe8da0-16d2-4efb-9bdb-a59e8214f3b6)

**Template Add authority.** `src/gateway/server-templates-add-authority.e2e.test.ts` adds a Template whose automation now runs in the main session, forces a run, and checks that the creator's operator authority and inference binding are restored and that a revoked operator is refused. On Linux, through `test/vitest/vitest.e2e.config.ts`, it passes 3/3 on main and 3/3 on this branch. (On Windows it fails the same way on main and on this branch, with a state conflict before Add starts, so that run says nothing about this change.)

**Checks run locally:** 162 affected tests passed (Template plan, apply, pending and schema tests, stateless Add automations, Gateway `templates.add` handlers, cron validation, protocol validators), plus `templates-add.inference-binding.test.ts` (12/12); `tsgo` for core and the templates test project; oxlint, oxfmt and `git diff --check`; test-lines ratchet (allocation 68) and assertion-safety ratchet.

